<DocsLayout title="Getting Started" description="Install the stack, run your first scenario and verify that routing works" section="Getting Started" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    The system ships as two Docker Compose stacks. <code>ids/</code> contains the reverse proxy, shop,
    honeypot pools, Redis, Suricata and Vector. <code>siem/</code> contains Elasticsearch, Kibana and the
    Vector aggregator. You can run both on one host or split them across two. The PyQt6 dashboard in
    <code>dashboard/</code> is the recommended way to configure, deploy and operate either shape.
  </p>

  <h2 id="paths">Choose a Path</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Dashboard (recommended)"
      href="#dashboard"
      icon="gauge"
      tag="Guided"
      description="Upload the project over SSH, edit .env, start and stop stacks, tail logs, open a container shell, run scenarios and export reports."
      meta="Handles local and remote targets · see the Dashboard Guide"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Manual Docker Compose"
      href="#manual"
      icon="terminal"
      tag="Minimal"
      description="Copy .env.example, set passwords, and bring up the edge stack directly. The SIEM stack is optional and can be added later."
      meta="One compose command"
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <h3>Docker and system packages</h3>
  <p>Every host that runs a stack needs Docker with the Compose plugin. The machine that runs the dashboard
  also needs <code>python3</code> with the <code>venv</code> module. For a remote target you also need an SSH key
  that can log in, and <code>rsync</code> on both machines. The dashboard checks for docker, Compose, ssh and
  rsync at startup and shows the matching command below in a banner if one is missing.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Distribution</th><th>Install command</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Arch / Manjaro</td><td><code>sudo pacman -S --needed docker docker-compose openssh rsync</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ubuntu / Debian / Mint / Pop!_OS</td><td><code>sudo apt install docker.io docker-compose-v2 openssh-client rsync</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fedora / RHEL / CentOS / Rocky / Alma</td><td><code>sudo dnf install docker docker-compose-plugin openssh-clients rsync</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>openSUSE</td><td><code>sudo zypper install docker docker-compose openssh rsync</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Other</td><td>Follow <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/">docs.docker.com/engine/install</a>.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The scenario scripts and the commands on this page also use <code>git</code>, <code>curl</code> and
  <code>jq</code>. Then let your user run Docker:</p>
  <pre><code>sudo systemctl enable --now docker.service
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"    # log out and back in afterwards
docker compose version             # confirms the Compose plugin</code></pre>

  <h3>Kernel settings</h3>
  <p>Without the first setting, Redis logs a memory-overcommit warning on every start. Elasticsearch needs the larger mmap limit on the SIEM host. No container can set these itself, so set them on each host. The dashboard's host-settings banner checks every host in use, including remote ones over SSH.</p>
  <pre><code>echo 'vm.overcommit_memory = 1' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-honeypot.conf
echo 'vm.max_map_count = 262144' | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.d/99-honeypot.conf
sudo sysctl --system</code></pre>

  <h3>Python environment for the dashboard</h3>
  <p>You normally don't need to do anything here. On its first run, <code>./run.sh</code> creates
  <code>dashboard/venv/</code>, installs PyQt6, and shows a progress window while it installs the rest of
  <code>requirements.txt</code>. To set the environment up by hand instead (for example on a headless build
  machine):</p>
  <pre><code>cd dashboard
python3 -m venv venv
./venv/bin/python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt</code></pre>
  <p>The dashboard uses PyQt6 WebEngine, which needs the usual Qt/Chromium libraries (on Arch: <code>nss</code>, <code>alsa-lib</code>, <code>mesa</code>, <code>libxkbcommon-x11</code>, <code>libxcomposite</code>, <code>libxdamage</code>, <code>libxrandr</code>, <code>libxtst</code>, <code>xcb-util-cursor</code>).</p>

  <h2 id="dashboard">Dashboard Setup</h2>
  <pre><code>cd dashboard
./run.sh</code></pre>
  <p>The first launch creates the venv (see above) and then opens the setup wizard. The wizard's steps are
  <strong>Welcome → Remote hosts (optional) → Edge .env → SIEM .env → Certificates (optional) → Done</strong>.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Welcome</strong>: shows a warning if docker, Compose, ssh or rsync is missing.</li>
    <li><strong>Remote hosts</strong>: mark <code>ids</code> and/or <code>siem</code> as remote and enter each
    one's SSH host, port, user, key and remote path. Leave both unticked to run everything on this machine.</li>
    <li><strong>Edge .env / SIEM .env</strong>: fill in each project's <code>.env</code>. A local file starts
    from <code>.env.example</code>. A remote file is fetched over SSH and saved back to the remote. Use
    <em>Generate</em> for the secrets (see <a href="#manual">the minimum values</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Certificates</strong>: <em>Generate all certificates now</em> creates the SIEM CA and service
    certificates and the edge nginx certificate. Do this before the first start.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>You can run the wizard again from <em>Connectors &amp; Config → Re-run setup wizard</em>. It keeps the values
  already in the <code>.env</code> files. For remote targets, copy the code to the host once from
  <em>Connectors &amp; Config</em>: run <em>Test connection</em>, then <em>Upload entire project…</em>
  (<code>rsync</code>, which skips backups, logs and volumes). After that, <em>Upload .env to remote…</em> pushes
  configuration changes alone.</p>

  <h3 id="first-run">First Run Walkthrough</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Clear the banners.</strong> Apply any commands shown in the dependency or host-settings banner
    above the pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Start the SIEM, then the edge.</strong> On <em>Workloads</em>, start <code>siem</code> and then
    <code>ids</code>. The progress bar counts one-shot jobs that exit with 0 as up. The first <code>ids</code>
    start seeds the shop and builds the initial pools, which takes a few minutes. The Honeypot layer dropdown
    on this page chooses between the two <a href="/docs/engine/layers">deception layers</a>; keep the default
    for a first run.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the topology.</strong> On <em>Global Visualization</em>, every node should be green
    (healthy, running or exited-ok). A red <strong>!N</strong> badge shows error lines in that container's
    log.</li>
    <li><strong>Check log shipping.</strong> <em>Log Pipeline</em> should report that the edge shipper is
    delivering to the SIEM's <code>vector_inbound</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Send a clean request, then an attack.</strong> On <em>Attack Simulation</em>, browse the shop
    (it should route to production), then pick the CVE-2024-27956 preset and press <em>Run pattern (curl)</em>.
    The Pattern analyzer tab shows each signal and the HONEYPOT decision. <em>Live Events</em> shows the same
    decision without needing the SIEM.</li>
    <li><strong>Follow the session.</strong> <em>Adversary Sessions</em> and <em>Pool State Store</em> show its
    score and pool. <em>SIEM Analytics</em> opens Kibana, logged in automatically, at the Attack Patterns
    dashboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean up.</strong> <em>Unpoison host IP</em> clears the reputation your own test runs built up for
    your host, so later visits are not diverted.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="admonition tip">
    <div class="admonition-title">Learning the dashboard</div>
    <p>Press <kbd>F1</kbd> on any page for its guide, or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> to jump to a page by name.
    The <em>Knowledge Base</em> page has eight guided labs that cover the steps above and more. The
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/dashboard">Dashboard Guide</a> describes every page.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="manual">Manual Setup</h2>
  <p>The edge stack is enough for a first run. The shop is published at <code>https://localhost/</code> with a self-signed certificate, so expect a browser warning. The command below brings up the default reverse-proxy deception layer (a separate decoy backend per session); see <a href="/docs/engine/layers">Honeypot Layers</a> for the lighter database-proxy alternative and how to choose between them.</p>
  <pre><code>cd ids
cp .env.example .env
$EDITOR .env                       # set real passwords first

docker compose up -d --build
docker compose ps
docker compose logs -f reverse_proxy</code></pre>

  <h3>Minimum .env values to change</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD</code></td><td>Root password for the production and honeypot databases.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>MYSQL_PASSWORD</code></td><td>Application user password for the production database.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>MYSQL_PASSWORD_POOL_2</code>, <code>MYSQL_PASSWORD_POOL_3</code></td><td>Per-pool database passwords. They must differ from the production password and from each other; pool isolation depends on this.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>REDIS_PASSWORD</code></td><td>Password for the Redis session store.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>SESSION_SIGNING_KEY</code></td><td>HMAC key for the signed <code>SERVERID</code> session cookie.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET</code></td><td>Gates the diagnostic <code>X-Route-Target</code> and <code>X-Risk-Score</code> headers. Leave unset in production so they fail closed.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="admonition warning">
    <div class="admonition-title">Secrets</div>
    <p>Never commit a filled <code>.env</code>. The uploaded copy on a remote host contains database, Redis and signing credentials, so restrict its permissions and rotate values if a host is rebuilt or shared.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Optional SIEM stack</h3>
  <pre><code>cd siem/certs/root-ca && ./gen_elk_certs.sh
cd ../../docker
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
  <p>Running both stacks on one host is supported for evaluation. The <a href="/docs/operations/two-host">two-host</a> and <a href="/docs/operations/single-host">single-host</a> pages describe the differences, and <a href="/docs/observability/siem">SIEM &amp; log pipeline</a> covers the data path.</p>

  <h2 id="verify">Verify the Stack</h2>
  <p>Work outward from the containers to the detection pipeline:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Containers are healthy.</strong> <code>docker compose ps</code> should show the proxy, production shop, pool manager, Redis, Suricata, Vector and the database services running. The first start also seeds the shop and builds the initial pools, which takes a few minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>The shop responds.</strong> Open <code>https://localhost/</code> and browse a product. A clean request should stay on production.</li>
    <li><strong>A scenario is detected.</strong> Run one of the scenario scripts, for example <code>bash testing/scenario_02_owasp_wstg.sh</code>. With the internal test secret configured, the response headers show the route and risk score.</li>
    <li><strong>A pool was assigned.</strong> Inspect Redis for the session and pool keys, or use the dashboard's Pool State Store, Adversary Sessions and Pool Scaling pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Events reached the SIEM.</strong> With the SIEM stack running, refresh the Kibana dashboards. Security events arrive through Vector; Suricata alerts arrive through <code>eve.json</code> ingestion.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="admonition tip">
    <div class="admonition-title">Diagnostic headers</div>
    <p>Set <code>INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET</code> and send a matching <code>X-Internal-Test-Auth</code> header to receive <code>X-Route-Target</code> and <code>X-Risk-Score</code>. Without the secret, the headers are never emitted — a blind pentest must not get the routing oracle for free.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cheatsheet">Command Cheat Sheet</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Task</th><th>Command</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Start the edge stack</td><td><code>cd ids &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rebuild after changes</td><td><code>docker compose up -d --build</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Follow proxy logs</td><td><code>docker compose logs -f reverse_proxy</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Open a Redis shell</td><td><code>docker compose exec session_store redis-cli</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Run all scenarios</td><td><code>bash testing/run_all_scenarios.sh</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Run Lua unit tests</td><td><code>cd reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua &amp;&amp; lua tests/test_pattern_rules.lua</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Run dashboard tests</td><td><code>cd dashboard &amp;&amp; python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Regenerate Kibana objects</td><td><code>python3 siem/kibana/build_dashboards.py --ndjson</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Export live dashboards</td><td><code>python3 siem/kibana/export_dashboards.py</code></td></tr>
  </table>

